.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Einstein (1907) treated each vibration as a quantum oscillator of frequency \nu, so its heat capacity depends on \Theta_{\text{vib}}/T with \Theta_{\text{vib}}=h\nu/k_B. VibrationalPartitionFunctionHarmonic&#x27;s heat capacity is this formula: it vanishes as T\to0 (the vibration is &quot;frozen out&quot;) and approaches the classical equipartition value k_B per mode only once T is comparable to \Theta_{\text{vib}}. The second plot shows why a stiff mode (like Einstein&#x27;s diamond) stays frozen out at temperatures where a soft mode already behaves classically.">

